    "inquisition": {
      "word": "INQUISITION",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "The act of inquiring; inquiry; search; examination; inspection; investigation. As I could learn through earnest inquisition. Latimer. Let not search and inquisition quail To bring again these foolish runaways. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Law) (a) Judicial inquiry; official examination; inquest. (b) The finding of a jury, especially such a finding under a writ of inquiry. Bouvier. The justices in eyre had it formerly in charge to make inquisition concerning them by a jury of the county. Blackstone.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(R. C. Ch.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A court or tribunal for the examination and punishment of heretics, fully established by Pope Gregory IX. in 1235. Its operations were chiefly confined to Spain, Portugal, and their dependencies, and a part of Italy.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To make inquisistion concerning; to inquire into. [Obs.] Milton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
