    "inquisitive": {
      "word": "INQUISITIVE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "Disposed to ask questions, especially in matters which do not concern the inquirer. A wise man is not inquisitive about things impertinent. Broome.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "Given to examination, investigation, or research; searching; curious. A young, inquisitive, and sprightly genius. I. Watts. inquisitive a habit. We are curious when we desire to learn something new; we are inquisitive when we set ourselves to gain it by inquiry or research. Prying implies inquisitiveness, and is more commonly used in a bad sense, as indicating a desire to penetrate into the secrets of others. [We] curious are to hear, What happens new. Milton. This folio of four pages [a newspaper], happy work! Which not even critics criticise; that holds Inquisitive attention, while I read. Cowper. Nor need we with a prying eye survey The distant skies, to find the Milky Way. Creech.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "A person who is inquisitive; one curious in research. Sir W. Temple.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
